Pin path /sys/fs/bpf/ferrum is writable by root on the node. Protecting it
requires BPF-LSM or an AppArmor profile applied on the node itself.
These manifests do not provide that and cannot: root on the node is not a
threat Kubernetes objects can fence off.

Base install binds only ferrum-agent-observe. Respond stays inert until
optional-respond.yaml is applied; that file carries both the ClusterRoleBinding
and a DaemonSet of the same name that replaces the base one — --role respond,
serviceAccountName ferrum-agent-respond, hostPID: true and CAP_KILL.

hostPID is in the respond variant only. The kernel reports tgid from the
initial pid namespace, so kill without hostPID would signal the wrong process;
granting it to an observe-only install would hand over every process on the
node for nothing.

The datapath ELF lives at /usr/share/ferrum/ferrum-ebpf-progs.bpf.o inside the
agent image and is passed as --bpf-elf in both DaemonSets. An agent built with
the attach feature exits rather than run without it; an agent built without
attach ignores the flag and reports Degraded. The image is built by the
Dockerfile at the root of this repository: ferrum-agent linked for musl with
--features attach,apiserver, which is the only combination that compiles the
cgroup sync into ferrum_cgroups, plus that ELF at that path.

Both DaemonSets mount /sys/kernel/tracing read-only. Attaching a tracepoint
means reading events/<category>/<name>/id out of tracefs, and so does the probe
that decides which hooks this kernel has. tracefs is a filesystem of its own
and no container runtime propagates it, so without the mount the probe can
establish nothing, every attach is tried, every one fails, and the agent parks
with "kernel attach failed, datapath is Degraded" on every node — the ELF, the
capabilities and the RBAC all correct. `ferrumctl lint-deploy` FD026 is a
finding on an attach build without it. hostPath type is Directory: a node whose
tracefs is somewhere else leaves the pod unschedulable and visible rather than
running an agent that can hook nothing.

CAP_BPF and CAP_PERFMON, and no CAP_SYS_RESOURCE. Kernels before 5.11 charge
the event ring and the cgroup hash against RLIMIT_MEMLOCK; the agent raises its
soft limit to the hard one before every load, which needs no capability, and
cannot raise the hard one, which would. Where that is not enough the load fails
and says so: the attach error names the limit it ran under, on stderr and in
status.json (containerMapError, and the container-map reason in
degradedReasons). It is never a silent degrade.

The respond ClusterRole grants pods delete only. pods/eviction and
networkpolicies were there for Isolate, which the agent does not implement and
refuses; they come back when it does.

Node state is a file, not a port: the agent rewrites status.json in
--export-dir (/var/log/ferrum) on every reload tick, beside events.jsonl, with
every counter it keeps, whether it is Degraded and the reasons for it. Read it
with the same cat as the events. It is written whole (temp file + rename), so a
reader never sees a partial one. There is no listening port and no probe on
these DaemonSets by design: the surface reports, it never acts, and a liveness
probe wired to the degraded state would restart a node through cold caches and
loop forever on a genuinely wrong ELF.

status.json is a snapshot, not a stream, and its ts is a freshness contract:
the file is rewritten every reload tick (--reload-ms, 1000 by default), so a ts
more than a few ticks behind wall-clock means the tick that should have
rewritten it did not run. Check ts before believing any other field. A tick
that cannot write the file removes the one already there rather than leave the
previous tick's snapshot asserting the state it had — /var/log/ferrum filling
up is the commonest cause of a failed write, and it is the same condition that
makes every event write fail, so the moment the file matters most is the moment
it would otherwise freeze on its last healthy answer. So: no status.json at
all, on an agent started with --export-dir, means the agent cannot publish, not
that it is not running. That state also sets Degraded with the reason "status
file unwritable", which rides the degraded field of every exported envelope and
the transition line on stderr; statusWriteFailedTotal in the next file that
does get written counts the publishes that failed.

Without --export-dir there is no status.json: the agent says so once on stderr
and then logs the whole status object there on every state change instead. That
is the whole reader in that configuration — the shipped DaemonSets both set
--export-dir, and the file is the surface this was built for.

--policy-name must name the policy the mounted bundle Secret is named for
(ferrum-bundle-cluster-<policy>). The FRMB carries no policy name, so nothing
at runtime can prove that join: a mismatch loads every PolicyException on the
node and applies none. `ferrumctl lint-deploy` FD024 checks it here, and the
agent says so in status.json if it holds waivers that can waive nothing:
waiversUnjoinedTotal counts the ones naming another policy, waiversInertTotal
also the expired and the malformed, and waiversUnjoined names them. One live
waiver does not excuse the rest — the count is per waiver.
